        dp = []
        dp[0] = True

        for i in range(1, len(s)+1):
            temp = False
            for j in range(i):
                if dp[j] and s[j:i] in wordDict:
                    temp = True
                    break
            dp.append(temp)
        
        return dp[-1]
            

        
    
# Problem: Word Break - determine if string s can be segmented into
# a space-separated sequence of one or more dictionary words.
#
# Core idea (both methods): use DP to track which indices of s are
# "reachable" - i.e., s[0:i] can be fully segmented into dictionary words.
#
# Method 1 (optimized): set + min/max word length pruning
#   - dp is a set instead of a list/array, so checking "is index j reachable"
#     is O(1) instead of O(n).
#   - We precompute minLen/maxLen (shortest/longest word in wordDict) to
#     prune the inner loop: any substring s[j:i] whose length falls outside
#     [minLen, maxLen] can never match a dictionary word, so we skip it.
#   - Trade-off: computing minLen/maxLen costs O(m) upfront (m = number of
#     words in wordDict), and wordDict itself is converted to a set for O(1)
#     membership checks. This adds some fixed overhead, but pays off overall.
#   - Time: O(n * k^2), where n = len(s), k = maxLen - minLen (bounded by
#     the range of word lengths). The inner loop runs k times, and each
#     substring check (slicing + hashing) also costs O(k). Since wordDict[i]
#     length is constrained to 1-20 per problem constraints, k is small and
#     effectively a constant, so this simplifies to roughly O(n).
#   - Space: O(n) in the worst case (dp can hold up to n reachable indices),
#     but the load factor / underlying properties of a hash table can make
#     actual memory usage somewhat worse than an equivalent array-based
#     structure of the same logical size.
#
# Method 2 (naive): boolean DP array
#   - dp[i] is a boolean indicating whether s[0:i] can be segmented.
#   - For each i, we check all j < i: if dp[j] is True and s[j:i] is in
#     wordDict, then dp[i] = True.
#   - Time: O(n^3 * m). The outer loop runs n times, the inner loop j can
#     run up to n times, and for each (i, j) pair, slicing s[j:i] costs up
#     to O(n) and checking membership against a list-based wordDict costs
#     O(n * m) (comparing against each of the m words, each comparison up
#     to O(n)). These costs multiply together, giving O(n^3 * m) overall.
#   - Space: O(n), since dp is always exactly n+1 boolean values.
